// In-depth battles, batch k: bagda, ainjalut, xiangyang, yamen, hakata, bachdang.
// Checked against the English (and, where it exists, Portuguese) Wikipedia articles on the Siege of Baghdad (1258),
// Ain Jalut, Kitbuqa, Qutuz, the Battle of Xiangyang, the Mongol conquest of the Song, the Battle of Yamen,
// Zhang Hongfan, Zhang Shijie, the Mongol invasions of Japan, the Battle of Koan, Genko Borui, the Battle of
// Bach Dang (1288) and the Mongol invasions of Vietnam, and the works those pages cite (Jackson, Biran, Amitai,
// Elleman, Turnbull, Conlan). Britannica pages could not be opened (access refused).

Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "ilcanato": "The Mongol state of Persia and the Middle East, founded by Hulagu. The name comes from il-khan, 'subordinate khan': its rulers obeyed the Great Khan in theory.",
  "grão-cã": "The supreme ruler of the Mongol Empire, above all other khans. Möngke and later Kublai were Great Khans.",
  "ismaelitas": "A branch of Shia Islam. The Nizaris of Iran were called 'Assassins' by their enemies, and lived in mountain fortresses such as Alamut.",
  "dawatdar": "Title of a high office at the Abbasid court; literally, the 'keeper of the inkwell'. In the defense of Baghdad, the dawatdar commanded the army.",
  "vizir": "The chief minister of a caliph or sultan.",
  "pontão": "A bridge made of boats or rafts tied side by side. It can be put up quickly, and it serves to cross a river or to close it off.",
  "mamelucos": "Slave soldiers, usually Turks from the Caucasus and the steppes, bought as boys and trained as elite horsemen. In Egypt, they seized power themselves in 1250.",
  "tumen": "A unit of the Mongol army, 10,000 men on paper. In practice it was often smaller.",
  "cavalaria pesada": "Armored horsemen on strong horses, used for the frontal charge.",
  "sultão do egito": "The head of the Mamluk state, in Cairo.",
  "trabuco de contrapeso": "A catapult that uses a large weight on one end of the beam to throw stones from the other. It hurls heavier projectiles farther than machines pulled by men.",
  "guarnição": "The body of soldiers that defends a city or fortress.",
  "navio de pás": "A boat driven by paddle wheels turned by people, rather than by sails or oars. The Chinese used them on rivers.",
  "cerco naval": "A blockade of a fleet or a port from the sea, to cut off its food and water.",
  "xogunato": "The military government of medieval Japan. The shogun, head of the samurai, held the real power, and the emperor was a figurehead. The Kamakura shogunate lasted from 1185 to 1333.",
  "samurai": "A Japanese professional warrior in the service of a lord. He fought on horseback with a bow and on foot with a sword.",
  "gokenin": "A direct vassal of the Kamakura shogun. In return for land and protection, he fought when called.",
  "tufão": "A violent tropical storm, with strong winds and rain, common in late summer in the seas of Japan and China.",
  "kamikaze": "'Divine wind', in Japanese. The name became tied to the typhoons that struck the Mongol fleets in 1274 and 1281, and centuries later was used for the suicide pilots of the Second World War.",
  "genkō bōrui": "The stone wall the Japanese built along the shore of Hakata Bay after the first Mongol invasion, about 20 km long.",
  "maré": "The regular rise and fall of the sea twice a day. In a river near its mouth, the tide changes the water level and uncovers what was hidden.",
  "estaca": "A pointed piece of wood driven into the ground. Here, into the riverbed, with an iron tip, to pierce the hulls of ships.",
  "junco": "A Chinese sailing boat with a wide hull and stiff sails braced with bamboo battens. Also a generic name for small boats of Southeast Asia.",
  "tributo": "A payment or gift that a weaker people sends to a stronger one to acknowledge its authority and avoid war."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  bagda: {
    war: "Hulagu's campaign in the Middle East (1253–1260)",
    hook: "In thirteen days, the Mongols brought down the caliphate that had ruled the Muslim world for more than 500 years.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1258, Baghdad was about 500 years old and was the capital of the [[Abbasids|abássidas]], the caliphs who ruled the Muslim world. It was one of the largest cities on the planet, with libraries and schools. But the garrison was, in the words of one historian, poorly trained and almost incapable of fighting.",
        "On the other side of the world, in 1251, Möngke had been proclaimed [[Great Khan|grão-cã]] of the Mongols. He sent his brother Hulagu west with two missions: to destroy the [[Ismailis|ismaelitas]] of Iran, the 'Assassins', and to secure the caliph's submission. Hulagu left Karakorum in October 1253.",
        { h: "The road to Baghdad" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1251", "Möngke is proclaimed Great Khan and sends Hulagu west."],
          ["October 1253", "Hulagu departs from Karakorum."],
          ["15 December 1256", "Alamut, the fortress of the Assassins, surrenders."],
          ["September 1257", "Hulagu demands that the caliph submit and hand over three senior ministers."],
          ["December 1257", "The Mongol armies enter the caliphate's territory from three sides."],
          ["January 1258", "They reach Baghdad."]
        ]},
        "Caliph al-Mustasim had agreed, at first, to send soldiers against the Assassins. Then he went back on it: his ministers convinced him that the request was a trick to empty the city of defenders. In September 1257 came Hulagu's direct threat, and the caliph's answer was defiant and disrespectful to the envoys. He sent only a small tribute and did not truly prepare the defense."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Hulagu" },
        "Brother of the Great Khan, Hulagu led the expedition. He had taken Alamut, the Assassins' fortress, in December 1256. According to the accounts, he consulted astrologers before attacking Baghdad: one of them, Husam al-Din, foretold disaster for whoever attacked it; the scholar Nasir al-Din al-Tusi foretold the opposite.",
        { h: "Caliph al-Mustasim" },
        "The last Abbasid caliph of Baghdad. He refused submission, answered Hulagu's threat with defiance and did not prepare the city's defense.",
        { h: "The vizier and the dawatdar" },
        "The [[vizier|vizir]] Ibn al-Alqami was a Shia in a mostly Sunni caliphate. Sunni writers later accused him of negotiating in secret with Hulagu, but that is only an accusation. The [[dawatdar|dawatdar]] was a high court officer who led the sortie against the Mongols in the open field.",
        { h: "The Mongol generals" },
        "Hulagu commanded the bulk of the army. General Baiju came from the west, passing through Mosul. Kitbuqa came from the south, and princes of the Golden Horde arrived on the right, across the Shahrizor plain. With the army came Guo Kan, with a corps of about a thousand military engineers. And there were vassal troops: Armenians, under King Hethum I, and Georgians. Hulagu's wife, Doquz Khatun, was a Christian, which later weighed in the sacking."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers are uncertain. For the Mongol army and its allies, the Portuguese Wikipedia gives 120,000 to 150,000 men, and the English one 138,000 to 300,000, counting auxiliaries. On the caliph's side, the maximum would have been about 50,000 poorly trained soldiers; the Portuguese Wikipedia gives 80,000. After the defeat in the field, about 30,000 were left to defend the city.",
        { h: "Three armies, one target" },
        "The Mongols advanced in three groups. On the right, the princes of the Golden Horde crossed the Shahrizor plain. On the left, Kitbuqa went through Khuzestan. In the center, Hulagu led the main army and sacked Kermanshah on 6 December 1257. Baiju reached Erbil and crossed the Tigris at Mosul, with the help of the local ruler, Badr al-Din Lu'lu'. By mid-January, he was beside the Nahr Isa canal, near Baghdad.",
        { h: "The sortie and the flood, 16 January" },
        "The dawatdar left the city with about 20,000 foot soldiers, faced a Mongol force under Sughunchaq and made it fall back. That same night, Baiju's men broke the dikes of the Dujayl canal and flooded the Abbasid camp, where the soldiers were celebrating. In the morning, the survivors were massacred. A few, among them the dawatdar, returned to the city. The army of Baghdad was practically finished.",
        { h: "The siege closes in" },
        "Kitbuqa crossed the river and reached the suburb of Karkh. Hulagu reached the eastern suburbs on 22 January, welcomed by the local Shia. In a single day, the Mongols raised a palisade and a ditch around the city, and built [[pontoons|pontão]] over the Tigris on both sides of the city, so that no one could flee by boat.",
        { h: "The wall falls" },
        "The assault began on 29 or 30 January, depending on the source. Arrows carrying messages were shot into the city, promising safety to those who surrendered. On 1 February, the Mongols opened a breach near the Ajami tower, in the southeast. The defenders drove them back, but over the next two days other breaches opened, and on 4 February the eastern walls were in Mongol hands.",
        "The dawatdar tried to flee by ship down the Tigris and lost three boats. Envoys were then sent to negotiate, among them the vizier Ibn al-Alqami and the patriarch of the Church of the East, Makkikha II. On 7 February, the dawatdar and the garrison commander were handed over and executed; the soldiers and civilians who came out unarmed were separated into groups and killed. On 10 February, the caliph himself surrendered, with his family and 3,000 dignitaries.",
        { h: "The sack" },
        "Hulagu ordered the population to leave the city after laying down their arms, and many who obeyed were killed. On 13 February the sack began, and it lasted about a week. Spared were the descendants of the Prophet, scholars, merchants who dealt with the Mongols and the Christians, through the intercession of Doquz Khatun, as long as they marked their doors. The rest of the city was robbed and put to the sword.",
        "On 15 February, Hulagu forced the caliph to show his treasures. On 20 February, al-Mustasim was executed along with his family and court. So as not to spill royal blood, according to the accounts, he was rolled in a carpet and trampled by horses. Hulagu left a governor and 3,000 soldiers in the city and departed on 8 March.",
        { note: "The caliph's death has two versions. The carpet one is the one most often told. Marco Polo and other Christian writers tell another, in which the caliph was locked in a cell with his treasures and starved to death. Modern historians treat this second one as a legend." }
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Outcome" },
        "The Abbasid caliphate ended, after more than 500 years. Mongol losses are unknown and were probably small. The city's are much debated: Hulagu spoke of two million dead in a letter to the king of France, a number historians consider clearly exaggerated. Muslim sources give 800,000 to 2 million. The English Wikipedia has 200,000 in its summary box, and the Portuguese one 100,000 to 1 million: the range is wide because no count exists. An epidemic after the sack, perhaps of plague, may have killed even more people. A physician of the time wrote that the bodies were thrown into the Tigris.",
        "The vizier al-Alqami was appointed again, but died less than three months later. The Mamluks of Egypt later installed a member of the Abbasid family in Cairo, with the title of caliph, but he was a figurehead. The center of power in the region became Tabriz, the capital of the [[Ilkhanate|ilcanato]].",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The Abbasid caliphate of Baghdad was over, and power in the region passed to the Mongol Ilkhanate. Many people count the fall as the end of the 'golden age' of Islam. But historians debate how large that end really was.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The black Tigris: a 16th-century account says so many books were thrown into the river that the water changed color and turned black with ink. The destruction of the House of Wisdom and of the libraries became the symbol of the disaster. But the historian Michal Biran points out that large libraries reopened for teaching less than two years after the siege." },
        { note: "Was it really the end? Biran and others argue that the fall was not the turning point it is usually made out to be. The long decline of Baghdad, according to her, came with the later sieges by Tamerlane (1393 and 1401) and by the Ottomans (1534). The sack, they say, was calculated, not a fit of rage." },
        { note: "The death tolls come from chroniclers and from a letter by Hulagu himself, and none results from a count. The safest thing to say is that it was a massacre of tens or hundreds of thousands of people, without precision." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  ainjalut: {
    war: "Mongol invasion of the Middle East (1256–1260)",
    hook: "An army of former slaves, the Mamluks, defeated the Mongols in a Galilean valley and changed the course of the Middle East.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1260, the Mongols seemed impossible to stop. They had destroyed the Assassins in Iran, sacked Baghdad in 1258 and taken cities in Syria. The Ayyubid dynasty of Damascus fell. Armenians of Cilicia and some Franks of Antioch had joined the Mongols. Of the Muslim world of the Middle East, only the Egypt of the [[Mamluks|mamelucos]] was still standing.",
        "The Mamluks were slave soldiers, mostly Kipchak Turks from the steppes, bought as boys and trained as elite horsemen. The Kipchaks supplied by the Golden Horde helped replenish Egypt's troops.",
        { h: "The road to the valley" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1258", "Baghdad falls to Hulagu."],
          ["12 November 1259", "Qutuz deposes the young sultan and takes the throne of Egypt."],
          ["Before July 1260", "Hulagu sends envoys to Cairo demanding submission. Qutuz executes them."],
          ["26 July 1260", "Qutuz leaves Cairo with the army."],
          ["Summer 1260", "Baibars takes Gaza; the Franks of Acre stay neutral."],
          ["3 September 1260", "Battle of Ain Jalut."]
        ]},
        "Hulagu sent envoys to Cairo with an order to surrender. Qutuz had them killed and displayed their heads at the Bab Zuweila gate in Cairo. It was an answer that left no room for negotiation. Meanwhile, Hulagu withdrew most of the army from Syria and left about one [[tumen|tumen]] (10,000 men on paper, usually fewer) under General Kitbuqa, plus allied troops."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Qutuz, sultan of Egypt" },
        "According to the sources, he was born a prince of the Khwarazmian empire, but was captured by the Mongols around 1231, taken to Damascus and sold as a slave. He ended up in Egypt, in the service of Sultan Aybak, and became deputy sultan in 1253. On 12 November 1259, with the Mongol threat growing, he deposed the 15-year-old sultan and took the throne, arguing that Egypt needed a strong leader. He earned the nickname 'Lion of Ain Jalut'.",
        { h: "Baibars, the vanguard commander" },
        "He commanded Qutuz's vanguard. He took Gaza, which was a Mongol outpost, and he was the one who played the bait in the battle. Less than two months later, Qutuz was assassinated. Medieval sources disagree on the details, but Western historians generally hold Baibars directly responsible, and he became sultan.",
        { h: "Kitbuqa, Hulagu's general" },
        "He was a Naiman, a people of Mongolia, a Christian of the Church of the East, and one of Hulagu's right-hand men. He is said to have sacked Sidon before the battle. The Mamluk chroniclers describe him as a brave warrior who refused to retreat.",
        { h: "The Franks of Acre" },
        "The Christians of Acre, remnants of the crusader kingdoms, were courted by both sides. The pope had forbidden an alliance with the Mongols, and the barons saw the Mongols as the more immediate threat. They stayed neutral: they let the Mamluks pass, camped nearby and supplied them on the plain of Acre."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers are disputed. Modern estimates give 15,000 to 20,000 Mamluks and 10,000 to 20,000 Mongols and allies. Some historians think the forces were similar; others, such as Hrbek, see on the Mongol side only a detachment, much smaller than the army of Egypt. The chronicler al-Yunini speaks of 100,000 Mongols, almost certainly an exaggeration.",
        "Kitbuqa's army had light cavalry and horse archers, [[heavy cavalry|cavalaria pesada]], infantry, Georgian troops, about 500 Cilician Armenians and contingents from the Ayyubid princes al-Ashraf Musa and al-Said Hasan.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Ain Jalut, 'spring of Goliath', is a spring that wells up at the northwest foot of Mount Gilboa, in the Jezreel Valley, in Galilee, about 15 km from Beisan (Baysan). Today it is called 'Ayn Harod. The Jalut stream ran along the foot of the mountain, with water and pasture for the horses, and the hill of Moreh gave a good view. The Mongols arrived first and chose the spot. The Mamluks knew the terrain better, and the hills of the region allowed troops to be hidden.",
        { h: "The bait and the trap" },
        { formation: true },
        "Qutuz hid the bulk of the army in the hills and sent Baibars ahead with a smaller force. For hours, Baibars attacked and withdrew, provoking the Mongols without taking risks, to preserve his men. In a stronger Mongol attack, his horsemen feigned one last retreat. Kitbuqa followed with all his forces, and one of the accounts calls this a serious mistake.",
        "When the Mongols reached the hills, the hidden troops came out, shot arrows and attacked the cavalry. The Mongols fought to break the encirclement. For the historian Timothy May, a turning point may have been the desertion of the Mongols' Syrian allies, but this is only one scholar's hypothesis.",
        "The Mamluk left wing was almost destroyed. Qutuz, according to the accounts, took off his helmet so his men would recognize him, prayed for victory and rushed into the fight with his unit, shouting 'Wa Islamah!' ('O my Islam!'). The Mongols were pushed toward Beisan and pursued. They managed to regroup and counterattack, but morale and terrain now favored the Mamluks, and part of the Mongol army had to fall back.",
        { note: "Kitbuqa's death has different versions. Some sources say he died fighting, after refusing to retreat. Others say he was captured, brought as a prisoner before Qutuz, defied him and was executed by a Mamluk veteran, Jamal al-Din Aqush. The English Wikipedia gives both versions in the same article. Both versions agree that he did not survive." }
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Outcome" },
        "It was a complete Mamluk victory. The Mongols lost their commander and most of the army that was in the region. After Ain Jalut, Hulagu had An-Nasir Yusuf, the last Ayyubid emir of Aleppo and Damascus, who was held prisoner, killed along with his brother. Damascus was retaken five days later, and Aleppo in less than a month. Ayyubid emirs allied with the Mamluks then defeated a Mongol force of 6,000 men at Homs, and the first Mongol expedition to Syria ended. At the end of 1260, Hulagu sent two tumens to Aleppo, which massacred many people, but went no further.",
        "Qutuz did not enjoy the glory: on 24 October 1260, on his way back to Cairo, he was assassinated at Salihiyah in a plot by the emirs. Baibars became sultan.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Many historians call it the first great Mongol defeat in a pitched battle and the end point of the Mongol advance to the west. It is a good explanation, but a simplified one. The Mongols returned to Syria in the following years and won some battles, but never conquered the region. The Mamluks won almost all of the later confrontations with the Mongols.",
        "There was also a political effect: Berke, khan of the Golden Horde, had converted to Islam and was angry over the sack of Baghdad. The conflict between the Mongols of the west and those of Iran prevented a larger campaign. Hulagu was defeated in the Caucasus in 1263 and died in 1265.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Why did Hulagu leave? Until the 1980s, historians said it was because of the death of Great Khan Möngke. But a letter from Hulagu himself, found by Meyvaert, gives other reasons: the grass for the horses had run out, and the Mongols usually sought fresher lands in the summer. A recent study links the withdrawal to the climate after the eruption of the Samalas volcano." },
        { note: "Cannons at Ain Jalut? The historian Ahmad al-Hassan argues that the Mamluks used hand cannons. Other specialists, such as Needham, Andrade and Ágoston, dispute this, and Khan dates the arrival of cannons in Mamluk Egypt to the 1370s." },
        { note: "The reference book on the war between the Mamluks and the Mongols is Mongols and Mamluks, by Reuven Amitai-Preiss." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  xiangyang: {
    war: "Mongol conquest of Song China (until 1279)",
    hook: "Six years of siege opened the door to southern China, and a new catapult decided the fight.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the 13th century, China was divided. In the north, the Mongols, who had already toppled the Jin empire, advanced on the south, where the Song dynasty reigned. To go from north to south, the greatest barrier was the Han River, a tributary of the Yangtze. On it stood two twin cities, one on each side: Xiangyang and Fancheng. Linked by a bridge of boats, they defended the entrance to the Yangtze valley and, with it, to the heart of the Song empire.",
        "The walls of Xiangyang were enormous: 6 to 7 meters thick, enclosing an area about 5 km wide. An earlier Mongol attack, in 1257, had failed, and the Mongol cavalry got stuck in the cities' double defenses.",
        { h: "From the start of the siege to the end" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1267", "Kublai Khan sends Aju and General Liu Zheng, a Chinese who changed sides, to attack the two cities."],
          ["1268", "The Mongols surround Xiangyang and Fancheng with forts."],
          ["1270 and 1271", "The Song try to relieve the cities by river and fail."],
          ["1272", "A Song force breaks the blockade and reaches the walls, but no one returns to tell of it."],
          ["Early 1273", "Fancheng falls."],
          ["14 March 1273", "Xiangyang surrenders."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Kublai Khan and Aju" },
        "Kublai was the Great Khan and wanted southern China. His general Aju led the siege. Aju asked Kublai for more men and siege engines, and the Great Khan sent 20,000 Chinese infantry soldiers and engineers from the Ilkhanate.",
        { h: "Liu Zheng, the general who changed sides" },
        "A general who had served the Song and went over to the Mongols. He advised starving the cities by cutting the supplies that came from Sichuan, instead of attacking the walls head-on.",
        { h: "Lü Wenhuan, commander of Xiangyang" },
        "The general who defended the city to the end. The power of the Lü family made the court doubt its loyalty. The emperor had kept Jia Sidao, the most powerful minister, from commanding the army, and sent Li Tingzhi, a rival of the Lü, to relieve the cities. But the Lü ignored Li's orders, and he managed only to bring in supplies for short periods.",
        { h: "The engineers of the Ilkhanate" },
        "Two engineers, Ismail and Al al-Din, came from Mosul, in present-day Iraq, and one source says that Abaqa, khan of the [[Ilkhanate|ilcanato]] of Persia, sent them by decree of Kublai, in 1272. The sources disagree on the exact names. They built the new machines under the direction of the Uyghur general Arikhgiya."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Mongols (Yuan) had about 100,000 men, according to the estimates cited by the English Wikipedia. The Song garrison would have been about 8,000. These were approximate numbers: neither the old nor the modern sources give firm counts. Kublai ordered the building of 5,000 ships in 1270 and 2,000 more in 1273.",
        { h: "The siege" },
        "Aju and Liu Zheng surrounded the cities with forts in 1268 and closed the land routes. The Song widened the moat and used clay and nets to cushion the projectiles, and the first Mongol machines were no use. On the river, the Yuan assembled a fleet of about 5,000 ships and blocked the Han with stone platforms. It was a long siege, in which the river was everything: whoever controlled it controlled the food.",
        { h: "Relief attempts that failed" },
        "From 1267 to 1271, several Song relief attempts failed. The English Wikipedia cites losses of 1,000 men in October 1270, 2,000 in August 1271 and most of a force of 3,000 the following month. In 1272, a force of 3,000 men, led by two officers with the surname Zhang, broke the blockade with about a hundred [[paddle-wheel ships|navio de pás]] and reached the walls. The problem was that no one managed to get out to send word. With no news of the success, the Song court sent no more reinforcements.",
        { h: "The new catapult" },
        "The engineers of the Ilkhanate built [[counterweight trebuchets|trabuco de contrapeso]], which threw projectiles of more than 300 kg about 500 meters, far beyond the old machines. About 20 were ready, according to the English Wikipedia, by March 1273. The same page dates the start of the bombardment to February, a small inconsistency that shows how approximate the dates at the end of the siege are. The Song also used gunpowder bombs, the 'thunder-crash bombs', fire arrows and fire lances, and the Mongols used the same bombs.",
        "Fancheng fell first, in early 1273, after bombardment and a cavalry assault. According to one source, the Mongols killed the entire population, as a warning to Xiangyang. The bombardment of Xiangyang began in February. Lü Wenhuan asked Emperor Duzong for reinforcements, who, on learning of the new machines, judged the city lost and sent nothing.",
        { h: "The surrender" },
        "On 14 March 1273, Lü Wenhuan surrendered. The Mongols appointed him governor of the two cities. He later led the garrison down the Yangtze, and the fortresses along the way surrendered too. He also persuaded other relatives to go over to the Mongol side.",
        { note: "Who won the siege: hunger or the machine? Some sources say the city already had little food in 1271, and that it was the blockade, more than the trebuchet, that defeated it. Others attribute the fall to the new catapult. The English Wikipedia notes that Marco Polo himself claimed a role, but the siege had already ended when he reached China, and this claim is disputed." }
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Outcome" },
        "Xiangyang and Fancheng were in Mongol hands, and the Han River and the Yangtze were open. The losses on each side were heavy, but the sources give no numbers. Other Song garrisons went over to the Mongol side, among them relatives of Lü Wenhuan. A Song attempt at a truce in 1275 came too late.",
        "Emperor Gong abdicated on 4 February 1276, and the capital, Lin'an, was taken. The Song dynasty held out a few more years at sea, until the Battle of Yamen, in 1279: six years after the fall of Xiangyang.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Xiangyang is an example of how a siege can decide an entire war. The Song had strong defenses and tried several times to relieve the city, but the court distrusted the Lü family, the relief attempts failed and the Mongols brought in from outside the engineers and machines they needed. It is also a good example of the exchange of technology across the Mongol Eurasia: the counterweight trebuchet, associated with the Middle East and the siege of Baghdad, helped decide a war in China.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The new catapult was known in China as 'hui-hui pao', the 'Muslim machine'. The counterweight design was the novelty: explosive bombs were already known there." },
        { note: "The numbers of the siege are approximate, and the English Wikipedia itself marks some of the losses as unsourced. The dates of the start and the end also vary: 1267 or 1268 for the start, and February and March 1273 for the outcome." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  yamen: {
    war: "Mongol conquest of Song China (until 1279)",
    hook: "The last Song fleet, with a 7-year-old emperor aboard, was chained together so it could not flee and ended up trapped inside its own defense.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1276, the Song capital, Lin'an, fell to the Mongols. Emperor Gong was captured, but the court did not surrender: his brother Zhao Shi was proclaimed emperor, and those loyal to the dynasty went south, fleeing along the coast. In 1277, after the fall of Fuzhou, the court went to Quanzhou, where General Zhang Shijie took ships from the Muslim merchant Pu Shougeng, who refused to lend them. Pu then massacred members of the imperial family and officials and went over to the Yuan.",
        "Zhao Shi died on Lantau Island, in Hong Kong, and the throne passed to his seven-year-old brother, Zhao Bing. Zhang Shijie took the court to Yamen, on the coast of Guangdong, to mount the last defense. In 1278, Wen Tianxiang, the great loyalist of the dynasty, was captured at Haifeng, and the last Song troops on land were finished.",
        { h: "From the palace to the sea" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1276", "Lin'an falls. Zhao Shi is proclaimed emperor."],
          ["1277", "The court flees from Fuzhou to Quanzhou. Pu Shougeng goes over to the Yuan."],
          ["1278", "Zhao Shi dies. Zhao Bing, aged 7, becomes emperor. Wen Tianxiang is captured."],
          ["12 to 17 March 1279", "Some Song desert, an attempt to break the encirclement fails, and the Yuan generals decide to attack."],
          ["19 March 1279", "Battle of Yamen."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Zhang Shijie, the Song admiral" },
        "He was born into a wealthy family in present-day Hebei. He began in the Mongol army, switched to the Song side, passed the civil service examination and came to handle civil, military and naval affairs. He was the last commander of the Song fleet. He is remembered, with Lu Xiufu and Wen Tianxiang, as one of the 'Three Loyal Princes' of the Song.",
        { h: "Lu Xiufu, the minister" },
        "Chief minister of the Song, he was with the boy emperor at Yamen. When he saw there was no way out, he made the choice that became famous.",
        { h: "Zhang Hongfan, the Chinese general of the Mongols" },
        "The irony of the battle: the one who destroyed the last Song fleet was a Han Chinese. Zhang Hongfan, born in present-day Hebei, was the son of Zhang Rou, who led local forces against the Mongols at the end of the Jin dynasty and went over to them in 1218. His family had never served the Song. Alongside him commanded Li Heng, of Tangut origin.",
        { h: "The Yuan's choice" },
        "Kublai's court rejected the use of catapults, for fear of provoking a flight of the Song fleet, and chose a naval siege to win by starvation."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Song fleet had more than 1,000 ships, but almost all were transports, escorted by warships. The English Wikipedia gives about 200,000 people, most of them officials, servants and civilians, and concludes that the fighting strength was tens of thousands. On the Yuan side, the sources differ greatly: 20,000 Chinese soldiers and more than 50 warships, according to one text; 300 to 700 warships, according to another.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Yamen is a strait on the coast of Guangdong, in southern China. It was a place with no base nearby to fall back to. And the lack of fresh water would weigh heavily.",
        { h: "The chained fleet" },
        { formation: true },
        "Zhang Shijie chained about 1,000 ships to one another, forming a solid mass, and put the emperor's boat in the center, so that no ship could escape. It was a floating fortress, but unable to maneuver or attack. A Yuan attempt with fire ships failed, because the Song had smeared mud on the hulls, making them fireproof.",
        "The Yuan closed off the bay and cut the fresh water and firewood on land. The Song soldiers were left with only dry food and seawater, and disease spread. Zhang Hongfan even demanded surrender three times, without success. He also chose not to use cannons, for fear that they would snap the chains and let the Song escape.",
        { h: "19 March: the attack" },
        "The most detailed version goes like this. The Yuan attacked with four fleets: Li Heng from the north and northwest, Zhang Hongfan from the southwest, and two others from the south and the west. Fog and rain covered Li's approach. The tide and the wind also helped, and the Yuan ships arrived from astern. The ships carried archer platforms, of seven or eight men each.",
        "Li's first attack cut the lashings of the Song fleet. Before noon, the Song fleet lost three ships. Li pierced the outer line, and two other squadrons destroyed the formation at the northwest corner. Then the tide turned and carried Li's ships north. The Song thought the Yuan had stopped and lowered their guard. That was when Zhang Hongfan, taking advantage of the northern current, attacked and captured the Song flagship, even though his own was hit by many arrows. Li's fleet returned to the fight. By late afternoon, it was all over, and the last Song fleet surrendered.",
        "Another version, in another text, is different: the Yuan played festival music, which supposedly lowered the Song's vigilance, and attacked at noon, with soldiers hidden under cloths, who were revealed when the battle horn sounded. Both versions agree on the main point: the chained fleet could neither move nor help itself.",
        { h: "The end" },
        "Zhang Shijie cut about a dozen ships out of the formation, with the best soldiers, to try to save the emperor, but the attempt was no use. Lu Xiufu, with no way out, jumped into the sea with Emperor Zhao Bing, and both drowned. Many officials and court ladies followed him."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Outcome" },
        "The Song dynasty was over. The English Wikipedia speaks of 'tens of thousands' of officials and women drowned, and of at least 100,000 dead in all, with no exact count. The History of Song says that thousands of bodies rose to the surface a week later. The emperor's body, according to one tradition, was supposedly found near Shekou, in Shenzhen, but the tomb was never located. Dowager Empress Yang also killed herself at sea, and Zhang Shijie buried her on the beach.",
        "Zhang Shijie supposedly escaped with his remaining ships and died in a storm a few days later, but this is uncertain. Some historians think the news of the storm may have been Mongol propaganda, since no trace of his body or of the fleet was ever found.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "With Yamen, Kublai's Mongols came to rule all of China. It was the first time all of China was ruled by foreigners. The English Wikipedia says the Yuan dynasty ruled for 97 more years, until the Ming. The boy emperor is still remembered today: in Teochew, a region of Guangdong, a 'patriotic soup' is made in his memory, and there are memorials to Zhang Hongfan, Wen Tianxiang, Lu Xiufu, Zhang Shijie and Zhao Bing.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The emperor's age: Zhao Bing was 7, according to the article about him, and 8, according to other texts." },
        { note: "The accounts of the battle do not agree on everything: the time of day, the fog, the music and the cloths appear in different versions. The number of Yuan ships also varies greatly (from more than 50 to 700), and the numbers are always approximate." },
        { note: "Some popular narratives say that Lu Xiufu carried the boy on his back when he jumped. The pages we consulted say only that he jumped with the emperor (one says 'holding him'), and one of them notes that it is not known whether it was he or others who decided the boy's death." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  hakata: {
    war: "The Mongol invasions of Japan (1274 and 1281)",
    hook: "An enormous fleet stopped before a stone wall, and a typhoon did the rest: the 'divine wind' was born.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Between 1266 and 1273, Kublai Khan sent several missions to Japan demanding that the country submit. The Kamakura [[shogunate|xogunato]] ignored them. In September 1275, five new envoys were beheaded in Kamakura on the orders of Hōjō Tokimune, who ran the shogunate. On 29 July 1279, five others were beheaded in Hakata.",
        "The first invasion was in 1274: a fleet landed at Tsushima, at Iki and in Hakata Bay. A storm ended the expedition, and the English Wikipedia says that 13,500 of the roughly 30,000 invaders did not return. The shogunate responded by building defenses.",
        { h: "The seven years between the invasions" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1274", "First invasion. A storm ends the expedition."],
          ["March 1276", "The shogunate begins building the stone wall in Hakata Bay."],
          ["1279", "The Yuan finish conquering Song China, which allows them to attack Japan on two fronts."],
          ["Autumn 1280", "Kublai assembles a council and plans the second invasion."],
          ["22 May 1281", "The Korean fleet departs from Korea."],
          ["15 August 1281", "A typhoon strikes the anchored fleet."]
        ]},
        "The shogunate had the Genkō Bōrui built, a stone wall of about 20 km along Hakata Bay, from Imazu to Kashii, about 2 meters high and thick, steep on the sea side and with a ramp on the land side. Each province of Kyushu paid a tax per rice field for the work. In 1281, only part of the wall was finished, but it was enough to prevent a landing at Hakata."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Hōjō Tokimune" },
        "He ran the shogunate, ordered the Mongol envoys killed and refused submission. About what he did in 1281, the pages we consulted say little, and we will not invent anything. The soldiers who faced the Mongols were samurai, such as the [[gokenin|gokenin]], direct vassals of the shogun.",
        { h: "Takezaki Suenaga and other samurai" },
        "Suenaga was a [[samurai|samurai]] from Kyushu whose deeds appear in the painted scrolls of the Mōko Shūrai Ekotoba. In one of them, the samurai is seen climbing onto a Mongol ship. According to the English Wikipedia, he attacked the stranded ships on 20 August. Other names from the chronicles: Kawano Michiari, who made raids; and Kusano Jirō, who, according to one chronicle, boarded a Mongol ship and took 21 heads.",
        { h: "Arakhan, Fan Wenhu, Hong Dagu" },
        "On Kublai's side, the supreme command belonged to Arakhan, who traveled with the southern fleet, under Fan Wenhu. The eastern fleet, from Korea, was commanded by Hong Dagu and Kim Bang-gyeong. Supply problems delayed the southern fleet."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The fleet's numbers vary greatly. The English Wikipedia gives 142,000 soldiers and sailors and 3,500 ships, but also speaks of 1,500 ships in another passage, without explaining the difference. Britannica speaks of 4,400 ships and about 140,000 men, and Japanese accounts of the time speak of more than 4,000 ships destroyed. Assembled, there would be two fleets: the Eastern Route, from Korea (a Korean text speaks of 26,989 Koreans), and the Southern Route, from southern China.",
        "Historians are doubtful. Thomas Conlan thinks 140,000 is an exaggeration of about ten times, which would give about 14,000 men. Morris Rossabi proposes about 70,000. Stephen Turnbull also finds the number exaggerated, but argues that the second invasion must have been three times larger than the first. As for the Japanese, the numbers are unknown.",
        { h: "June: the islands" },
        "The eastern fleet left Korea on 22 May. It attacked Tsushima on 9 June (8, according to another Wikipedia page) and Iki on 14 June. On the death of the defender Shōni Suketoki, the sources differ: the History of Yuan says he died at Tsushima, but Turnbull places it in a July fight. On 23 June, Hong Dagu and Kim Bang-gyeong disobeyed the order to wait at Iki and proceeded to Japan a week before the expected arrival of the southern fleet, on 2 July.",
        "On 25 June, about 300 ships attacked Nagato and were repelled. Other ships landed near Hakata, but the wall did not let them through. Unable to land at Hakata, the Mongols anchored at the island of Shikanoshima.",
        { h: "July: the raids" },
        "For weeks, small boats of samurai attacked the anchored fleet, by day and by night. The Mongols fell back to Iki on 30 June. On 16 July, a Japanese attack on Iki (of about 10,000 men, according to one of the sources) led the Mongols to fall back to Hirado. The first ships of the southern fleet arrived on 16 July, and on 12 August the two fleets were ready. The Mongols took the island of Taka (Takashima), and the situation stalled for about two weeks around Hakata.",
        { h: "12 to 15 August: the typhoon" },
        "On 12 August, the Japanese night attacks continued. The Mongols lashed the ships together and planked them over. On 15 August, a [[typhoon|tufão]] struck the anchored fleet, coming from the west, and lasted two days. Koreans and southern Chinese tried to flee to Imari Bay, where many ships broke apart.",
        "Thousands of soldiers were left adrift or ended up on the beach, and the Japanese killed those they found, except the southern Chinese, seen as having been forced to fight. A Chinese survivor told that Fan Wenhu took the best remaining ships and left, abandoning more than 100,000 men. Trapped on Taka Island, after three days the Japanese attacked them and captured tens of thousands (20,000 to 30,000, according to one source). Taken to Hakata, Mongols, Koreans and northern Chinese were killed. The southern Chinese were spared, but enslaved."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Outcome" },
        "The losses were enormous. A Korean source says that 7,592 of the 26,989 Koreans of the Eastern Route did not return. Chinese and Mongol sources indicate 60% to 90% casualties. The page on the Battle of Kōan speaks of about 80% dead, and the summary box of the English Wikipedia puts the losses above 120,000 men. On Japanese losses, the sources say little.",
        "The Yuan lost most of their navy, and Korea lost timber and shipyards. The shogunate came out worse: with no new lands conquered, it could not reward the [[gokenin|gokenin]], and its prestige fell. The pirates, the wokou, increased their raids on China and Korea in the following years.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The typhoon reinforced the Japanese belief that the country could not be invaded, and the name [[kamikaze|kamikaze]], the 'divine wind', went down in history. Centuries later, it was reused for the suicide pilots of the Second World War. But the typhoon was not the only story: the wall prevented the landing at Hakata, and the samurai attacks on the fleet, night after night, pressured the anchored fleet until the storm arrived.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The bombs in the scrolls: the historian Thomas Conlan argues that the images of explosive bombs in Suenaga's scrolls were added in the 18th century, and therefore do not show what was seen in 1281." },
        { note: "River ships? The Portuguese Wikipedia records the idea that many ships of the fleet were light river vessels and could not withstand the open sea, and that some seagoing ships were deliberately built with flaws. This is the opinion of some scholars, not a consensus." },
        { note: "Archaeology: from a shipwreck near Takashima, underwater archaeologists recovered ceramic bombs, the tetsuhō. X-rays showed they had gunpowder and pieces of iron inside." },
        { note: "Dates vary between the sources by a day or two, and the totals of ships also vary, which is why ranges appear and not exact numbers." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  bachdang: {
    war: "Third Mongol invasion of Đại Việt (1287–1288)",
    hook: "The tide rose, hid the stakes, fell, and the river did the rest.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1285, Đại Việt, the Vietnam of the Trần dynasty, had repelled a Mongol invasion. Kublai's envoys demanded that the king go in person to the Mongol court, and he refused. Đại Việt had also denied passage to General Toghon, who wanted to attack the neighboring kingdom of Champa. The invasion of 1285 ended with the Yuan out of Thăng Long. At Chương Dương, for example, General Sogetu was killed. The sources disagree on why: the History of Yuan speaks of the Mongols' inability to fight well there, and a chronicle says the city was retaken by the Vietnamese.",
        "Kublai did not give up. In 1286, he named Trần Ích Tắc, a Vietnamese prince who had gone over to the Yuan, as 'king' of Đại Việt, to replace King Trần Nhân Tông. He also canceled the plan to invade Japan in order to concentrate everything on the south. In October 1287, a third invasion began.",
        { h: "The third invasion" },
        { timeline: [
          ["October 1287", "Toghon advances by land from Guangxi and Yunnan; Omar commands the fleet."],
          ["17 December 1287", "The fleet departs from Qinzhou."],
          ["January 1288", "Vietnamese forces, under Trần Khánh Dư, destroy Zhang Wenhu's supply fleet."],
          ["February 1288", "The Yuan take Thăng Long (Hanoi), but find no food."],
          ["5 March 1288", "Short of food, the Yuan fall back to Vạn Kiếp."],
          ["30 March 1288", "Toghon orders the retreat to China."],
          ["9 April 1288", "Battle of the Bạch Đằng River."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Trần Hưng Đạo, Vietnamese commander" },
        "He was the head of the forces of Đại Việt. He prepared the terrain from March onward, studied the tides and used the same method as Ngô Quyền, who in 938 had defeated a fleet of the Chinese kingdom of Southern Han on the same river.",
        { h: "Trần Nhân Tông, the king" },
        "King of Đại Việt, he refused to go to Kublai in person. After the war, he accepted nominal Yuan overlordship to avoid new attacks, but never went to the court.",
        { h: "Toghon and Omar, the Yuan commanders" },
        "Toghon, a Mongol prince, commanded the land army. Omar, with Fan Yi and Mahmud, commanded the fleet. It was Omar who went down the river with the ships, and he was the one who ended up captured."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The English Wikipedia gives the Yuan about 100,000 to 170,000 men in total: 70,000 Mongols, Jurchens and Han Chinese, 6,000 from Yunnan, 1,000 former Song soldiers, 6,000 from Guangxi, 17,000 Li from Hainan and 18,000 sailors. Omar's fleet would have 18,000 soldiers, tens of thousands of sailors, 70 cargo ships and 500 warships. On the Vietnamese side, a Vietnamese source gives 50,000 men, without detail. None of these numbers originates in a reliable count.",
        { h: "The Yuan's mistake: Thăng Long without food" },
        "In January, Vietnamese forces under Trần Khánh Dư attacked Zhang Wenhu's supply fleet, near Hạ Long Bay, and forced it back to Hainan. The monsoons scattered other cargo ships. The Yuan occupied Thăng Long in February, but found no food for the army. On 5 March, Toghon left Hanoi for Vạn Kiếp. About 25 days later, he decided to return to China.",
        { h: "The terrain: the river and the tide" },
        { formation: true },
        "The Bạch Đằng runs through Yên Hưng (Quảng Ninh) and Thủy Nguyên (Hải Phòng) to the sea. Trần Hưng Đạo had iron-tipped [[stakes|estaca]] driven into the bed of the Chanh, Kênh and Rút rivers, tributaries north of the Bạch Đằng. The ambush point was the Ghềnh Cốc reef: at low [[tide|maré]], the stakes would be exposed and would trap the ships. Small Vietnamese flotillas hid behind Ghềnh Cốc, Đồng Cốc, Phong Cốc and in nearby rivers, and the infantry was posted on both banks.",
        { h: "9 April" },
        "Toghon retreated by land, pursued as far as Lạng Sơn. Omar and Fan Yi went down the Bạch Đằng, the same way they had used to come in. In the early morning, with the tide high, the fleet, escorted by infantry, entered the trap. A small force of Vietnamese junks came out, attacked and withdrew. The Yuan fleet followed. With the tide falling, the stakes appeared, and the trapped ships were destroyed by the junks that returned.",
        "Thousands of Yuan soldiers drowned or were killed. Fan Yi tried to flee, jumped into the river and was killed by the Vietnamese. The fighting lasted from sunrise to sunset. Omar was captured. A few days later, Zhang Wenhu entered the river with the transport fleet, unaware of the defeat, and was also destroyed. Only he and a few soldiers escaped. Toghon fled through the forest with few men (according to another page, struck by a poisoned arrow on 10 April). Most of his army was killed or captured."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Outcome" },
        "The English Wikipedia gives 4,000 to 4,500 dead on the Vietnamese side and 80,000 to 90,000 dead or captured on the Yuan side. As for ships, the text says the Vietnamese captured 400, and the summary box speaks of 500 sunk or captured. The two counts do not match.",
        "Kublai, furious, exiled Toghon to Yangzhou for the rest of his life. The two sides agreed to exchange prisoners, and in 1289 King Trần Nhân Tông agreed to return Omar. Trần Hưng Đạo opposed it and had the ship carrying the prisoner sunk at sea. Đại Việt later accepted a nominal relationship of [[tribute|tributo]] with the Yuan, which lasted until the end of the dynasty. The English Wikipedia also says that the defeat ended Mongol ambitions in Southeast Asia, but marks that sentence as unsourced.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Bạch Đằng is one of the great memories of Vietnamese history. It is also a good example of how to use terrain and tide against a larger enemy, and of a trick that was repeated: the stakes of 938 were used again in 1288.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Who won? Many historians treat the wars of the Yuan dynasty against Đại Việt as a Mongol success, because in the end a tribute relationship was created. For Vietnamese historiography, they were a great victory. Both points of view have a basis." },
        { note: "The numbers of ships and men vary between the pages (400 or 500 ships taken, 80,000 or 90,000 dead). The English article relies mainly on a study by Elleman (2012), and the Vietnamese numbers appear as 'Vietnamese sources'. The version in which the enemy fleet was attacked with fire, common in popular accounts, does not appear in the pages we consulted." },
        { note: "Emperor Trần Minh Tông and the writers Phạm Sư Mạnh and Nguyễn Trãi wrote poems about the place." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

